this is a production of cornell  university  hello my name is carl scamenti from the  cornell turf grass program  and today i'm going to talk about a  brief case study on a project we're  working on  called the environmental results program  that aims to increase adoption of best  management practices  on golf courses in new york  and so part of this project is to  implement bmps  that will protect water quality  at actual facilities in our region  and so today's uh bmp is going to be at  pearl lakes golf course  uh which is a nine-hole golf facility up  in skinny atlas new york  uh it's a kind of a par-3 short course  so very accessible community-based um  very affordable for the for the people  in our region  and what we did up there is some basic  soil chemical testing  um so the idea behind soil testing on  golf courses is to  first of all establish baseline soil  nutrient levels uh but then to use that  data to build an efficient fertilizer  program  and when we say efficient we mean  applying only the nutrients that are  necessary for  turfgrass health and not excess  when you apply excess nutrients and  fertilizer  um you're not only wasting money but  you're increasing the risk of runoff and  leaching of those nutrients that can get  into water bodies and cause issues  um  so just some basics on what we did out  of pearl lakes  what did we use to soil sample  just a basic soil probe a container to  put our samples in  ziploc bags that's what you put your  samples in to send them off to the lab  lab with a sharpie  and then just some sand to back fill the  holes and smooth everything over once  we're done  and then some more fundamentals  if you're new to soil testing  how many greens do you want to do if you  have 18 greens at your facility  you may only want to do a couple so  choosing a representative sample  for your golf course that means a couple  of your best greens couple your worst  greens  and a couple in between  we'll give you a good sample  if you can't do every green at your golf  course  about eight to ten samples per surface  uh depends on the size of the green and  uh but make sure you're you're spacing  those out evenly across the the green  again representative sample there  and consistent sampling depth so  you'll notice um on our soil sampling  device here we've got a little piece of  duct tape that just makes sure that  every time we sample we're getting the  same depth for consistency's sake  again consistency  if you're going to do this annually  taking samples the same time of year  and using the same lab every single time  it's going to ensure that consistency  year over year  all right so the results you'll send  your samples in and you will get  something like this  a lot of numbers flying around here what  do they mean  uh what is a good number what is a bad  number  that's where you need an interpretation  method  and the one we recommend is the minimum  levels of sustainable nutrition  so that is mlsn for short and this is a  very simple way of interpreting soil  test results  all you need is enough right so the mlsn  levels are based on  thousands of of turf grass samples  across the world  and each number here is just the  concentration in the soil that is the  minimum level that will maintain healthy  turf  so if we're looking at potassium 37  parts per million is that minimum level  if we get a soil test back that says 50  parts per million  great we're good to go we are above that  37 number the turf has all it needs  now if we had 100 right it's it's it's  really no different according to mlsn we  still have enough we are still above  that minimum level  there's no optimum just being above that  is the important thing here  um so the only time you need fertilizer  for any of these elements is if you are  below the minimum level right so if we  have 20 parts per million  okay then we have to go in  and we can apply some potassium to get  us above that mlsn  um so looking at the results from pearl  lakes we can see  pretty obviously this phosphorus column  is is the one highlighted in red  every green is below the mlsn level for  phosphorus um  this tracks with some uh historical  observations where seeding wasn't super  successful  on some of the putting greens um  so looking at this data we'd say  phosphorus is below the ml sun level we  need to go in and apply some fertilizer  so that the turf grass has what it needs  to be healthy  now how much fertilizer we apply that's  a little bit more a detailed discussion  we have a case study link below that  goes through the calculations you'll  need  to figure out exactly how much  phosphorus  um but what we're going to do here is  just talk about what the recommendations  for mlsn were  versus what conventional more  traditional recommendations would be  so right here we have  the mlsn interpretation versus  slan which again is more conventional  method  um if we're looking at just phosphorus  here on the left uh mlsn recommended  about a pound per thousand  of phosphorus fertilizer  slain recommended more than twice that  right so we're looking across the whole  golf course 40 pounds to about 100  pounds  and we see even a more exaggerated  example looking at potassium  mlsn recommended zero right and slan  recommended almost 200 pounds across the  entire golf course  four and a half pounds per thousand  um this is pretty common any  interpretation method other than mlsn is  going to recommend more fertilizer  and if you're wondering well does that  mean the grass is going to look better  um what we're finding is there's  research research showing now that mlsn  is providing the same turf turf grass  quality the same results  with much less fertilizer  and that's kind of the point  looking at mlsn as an interpretation  method  you're going to get the same turfgrass  quality with less fertilizer and for you  that means  less money spent  and also it means a reduced risk of  possibly contaminating your water your  water bodies around the golf course  property  with excess nutrients  so thanks for listening to this case  study at pearl lakes golf course  uh again we'll have our case study link  below that goes a little bit more in  detail about  how you use um the mls send to calculate  fertilizer requirements  uh we've hoped you learned something and  look forward to seeing you soon this has  been a production of cornell university  on the web at cornell.edu
 

